s":"heart"},{"surface":"அவன்","parts":[{"text":"அவன்","lemma":"avaṉ","gloss":"he/his"}],"gloss":"his"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"கழிமென்","parts":[{"text":"கழிமென்","lemma":"கழிமென்","gloss":"very soft"}],"gloss":"very soft","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"சாயல்","parts":[{"text":"சாயல்","lemma":"cāyal","gloss":"grace/nature"}],"gloss":"grace"},{"surface":"காண்தொறும்","parts":[{"text":"காண்","lemma":"kāṇ","gloss":"see"},{"text":"தொறும்","lemma":"toṟum","gloss":"every time"}],"gloss":"every time I see"},{"surface":"நினைந்தே","parts":[{"text":"நினைந்து","lemma":"niṉai","gloss":"thinking of"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ē","gloss":"emphatic particle"}],"gloss":"thinking of"}]}],"literal":"Bright-bangle-wearing women of the playing group / when digging the dark mud ploughed by wild boars / the fishy-smelling eggs laid by tortoises / along with honey-smelling water lily tubers obtain / of the beautiful water-sluice that sounds with a rushing noise / the chief of great, excellent Vilaṅkai, who wears the garland of words of the poor, who has a small lute, Nalliyakkōṭaṉ / you possess, may you live, my destined fate! / Like those who disregarded the clear water of the cool mountain pool of Pāri's Paṟampu hill because it was in their own village / the days that passed without seeing him, / my heart grieves on the following days, / thinking of his very soft grace every time I see him.","translation":"The young women with bright bangles, playing in their group, dig up the dark mud ploughed by wild boars and find fishy-smelling tortoise eggs along with honey-scented water-lily tubers. Such is the fertile land of Nalliyakkōṭaṉ, the lord of great Vilaṅkai with its rushing water-sluices, who wears the garland of praise sung by the poor, and who cherishes the small lute. O my destined fate, may you live long for bringing me to him! Just like people who live in the same village as the cool, clear mountain pool of Pāri's Paṟampu hill and take its water for granted, I once let days pass without seeing him. Now, my heart grieves for those lost days, whenever I behold his exceedingly gentle grace and think of him.","modern":"விளையாடும் கூட்டத்தைச் சேர்ந்த பிரகாசமான வளையல்களை அணிந்த பெண்கள், காட்டுப்பன்றிகள் உழுத கரிய சேற்றைக் கிளறும்போது, ஆமைகள் இட்ட புலால் நாற்றமுடைய முட்டைகளையும், தேன் மணம் வீசும் ஆம்பல் கிழங்குகளையும் சேர்த்தே பெறுகின்றனர். இத்தகைய நீர் பாயும் மதகுகள் சத்தமிடும் வளமான பெருமாவிலங்கை நாட்டின் தலைவனும், வறியவர்களின் சொற்களை மாலையாக அணிபவனும், சிறிய யாழையுடைய பாணர்களை ஆதரிப்பவனுமாகிய நல்லியக்கோடனை நான் அடையும்படி செய்த என் விதியே, நீ வாழ்க! பாரியினுடைய பறம்பு மலையின் குளிர்ந்த சுனையின் தெளிந்த நீரைத் தங்கள் ஊரிலேயே இருப்பதால் அதன் அருமை தெரியாமல் அலட்சியம் செய்தவர்களைப் போல, அவனைப் பார்க்காமல் கழித்த நாட்களை நினைத்து, அவனது மிக மென்மையான அருங்குணத்தைக் காணும்போதெல்லாம் என் நெஞ்சம் வருந்துகிறது.","explanation":"The poet Puṟattiṇai Naṉṉākaṉār sings this poem in praise of the patron Oymāṉ Nalliyakkōṭaṉ. Having received his generous gifts, the poet expresses deep gratitude to his own destined fate for bringing him to such a noble lord. He describes the fertile land of Vilaṅkai, where girls playing in the mud easily find both tortoise eggs and water-lily tubers, symbolizing the effortless abundance of the patron's realm. The poet then regrets the days he spent without visiting Nalliyakkōṭaṉ, comparing his past ignorance to villagers who neglect the sweet, cool waters of the famous Paṟampu hill pool simply because it is close to them. This comparison highlights how people often fail to appreciate great treasures when they are easily accessible, reflecting the poet's self-reproach for not seeking Nalliyakkōṭaṉ sooner.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.5],[1.1,1.3],[1.3,1.8],[2.25,2.48],[2.48,2.7],[2.7,3.2],[3.2,3.7],[3.7,4.1],[4.1,4.7],[4.7,5.4],[5.4,5.7],[5.7,6.9],[6.9,7.2],[7.2,7.4],[7.4,8.1],[8.1,8.77],[8.77,9.6],[9.6,10.1],[10.1,10.6],[10.6,11.41],[11.41,11.88],[11.88,12.3],[12.3,12.5],[12.66,13.43],[13.74,14.3],[14.3,1